TITLE='Authentic title "The Biggest Financial Traps Men Fall In Their 40\'s (Nobody Warns You)" targets a gendered, age-specific audience with a warning frame. The script lists eight traps (peak-earnings illusion, status truck, youth sports, whole life, toys, trading/betting, forever mortgage, catch-up delusion). TYPICAL (0.85x) against peers that include "Wealthy Retirees Stopped Buying" (78K) and "$100 Per Month" (28K). Association only; no CTR evidence.'
THUMB='Registered maxresdefault 1280x720 (HTTP 200, real image). Three-panel triptych. Left: "MIDLIFE CRISIS", the recurring cartoon man pointing at a red sports car with a "$250,000" tag. Centre: "EGO SPENDING", the man in sunglasses, monogram jacket, gold chain and watches, pulling out empty pockets. Right: "BAD DIVORCE", the man with head in hands before a house split down the middle with cash flying. The script never discusses divorce, and its vehicle trap is a $66,000 pickup, not a $250,000 sports car, so the packaging overstates and adds an unaddressed theme. At 320x180 all three headlines remain legible.'
SCRIPT='An eight-trap listicle for men in their 40s: garage hook, peak earnings and Vanguard savings, status truck, youth sports, whole life insurance, toys (boats, RVs), trading and sports betting, the forever mortgage and HELOCs, the catch-up delusion, a free-community promotion and a close. Verified: Vanguard 45-54 median $67,796 (2025 edition), AGA $13.71B bettor losses. Reproduces: truck $75,600 and $271K, sports $89K, whole life 19x and $248K, boat $193K, betting $43K, catch-up $1.27M. Errors: catch-up figure deflated twice (7% real then inflation-adjusted to ~$700K); AGA handle $149.90B not $147.91B; Barber-Odean return gaps garbled; median applied to "working men"; "$43,000 is a week of retirement income". Thumbnail promises a divorce segment that does not exist. The stored ending is complete.'
BEATS=['Hook: 43-year-old in the garage.','Trap 1: peak earnings illusion and Vanguard balances.','Trap 2: status truck and opportunity cost.','Trap 3: youth sports and scholarships.','Trap 4: whole life vs term.','Trap 5: boats, RVs and depreciation.','Trap 6: trading ego and sports betting.','Regrounding: extra million from inaction.','Trap 7: forever mortgage and HELOCs.','Trap 8: catch-up delusion.','Community promotion.','Close: everyone profits; compounding is symmetrical.']
